class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {

        // i -> pointer for string s
        // j -> pointer for pattern p
        int i = 0, j = 0;

        // star -> position of last '*' seen in pattern
        // match -> position in string where '*' started matching
        int star = -1, match = 0;

        // Iterate through the string
        while (i < s.length()) {

            // Case 1: characters match OR pattern has '?'
            // '?' matches exactly one character
            if (j < p.length() &&
               (p.charAt(j) == s.charAt(i) || p.charAt(j) == '?')) {

                i++; // move string pointer
                j++; // move pattern pointer
            }

            // Case 2: pattern contains '*'
            else if (j < p.length() && p.charAt(j) == '*') {

                // store position of '*'
                star = j;

                // store position of match in string
                match = i;

                // move pattern pointer to next char
                j++;
            }

            // Case 3: characters don't match but we saw '*' before
            else if (star != -1) {

                // go back to position after '*'
                j = star + 1;

                // extend the match of '*'
                match++;

                // move string pointer
                i = match;
            }

            // Case 4: mismatch and no '*' to adjust
            else {
                return false;
            }
        }

        // If pattern still has remaining '*', skip them
        while (j < p.length() && p.charAt(j) == '*') {
            j++;
        }

        // If we reached end of pattern, match is successful
        return j == p.length();
    }
}

Intuition (Simple)

Think of * as elastic:

When we see *, we remember its position

If a mismatch happens later, we expand * to cover more characters

So we backtrack only to the last *, not the whole string.

Example Dry Run
s = "adceb"
p = "*a*b"

Steps:

* matches ""
a matches a
* matches "dce"
b matches b

Result → true

